  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nFOG:\n\nA decoupling boundary layer in vicinity of a weak surface trough \namidst a moist airmass and moist soils should support patchy to \nareas of fog later tonight into Sunday morning across central and \nnorth-central Kansas, mainly west of a line extending from Lincoln \nto Lyons. Evening-night shifts will need to monitor for possible \ndense fog.\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES:\n\nTHROUGH MID-AFTERNOON...Expecting areas of rain and embedded rumbles \nof thunder to continue exiting eastern KS to the east this \nafternoon, just ahead of shortwave energy traversing the region. \nBrief heavy rain will be the primary hazard.\n\nLATE AFTERNOON--EVENING...By late this afternoon and early evening, \ncan't rule out a few isolated thunderstorms generally along/west of \nInterstate 135, just ahead of a subtle surface trough. Convergence \nand upper forcing look rather weak (could even prove subsident in \nwake of current wave), and model soundings suggest the potential for \nsome capping issues, so chances are low. However, if an isolated \nstorm can get going, strong instability coupled with marginal deep \nlayer shear would support a localized hail/wind threat.\n\nTHIS EVENING--EARLY SUNDAY...Modest 850-600mb warm advection and \nmoisture transport ahead of a weak mid-level trough approaching from \nthe northwest should support a modest uptick in shower/thunderstorm \ncoverage (although probably not widespread), generally east-\nsoutheast of the KS Turnpike. Some directional component to the deep \nlayer shear coupled with modest elevated instability may support a \nlocally strong storm along with brief heavy rain.\n\nSUNDAY LATE AFTERNOON--EVENING...May see a few isolated \nthunderstorms once again across portions of central, south-central, \nand southeast KS, in vicinity of a weak surface trough amidst a \nmoist and weakly capped airmass. Once again, decent instability and \nmarginal shear may support a locally strong to severe thunderstorm, \nbut activity should remain fairly isolated. There is some CAM \nsupport for this isolated activity.\n\nTUESDAY--WEEK'S END...Attention then turns to the potential for a \nprolonged period of off-and-on showers/thunderstorms, as a strong \nand slow-moving upper trough approaches from the west. Precipitable \nwaters are forecast to be a staggering 4-5+ standard deviations \nabove average, owing to remnants of Eastern Pacific tropical systems \ngetting absorbed into this approaching system. Additionally, there \nwill be some instability present as well. Consequently, a multi-day \noff-and-on heavy rainfall event looks increasingly likely across the \nregion, with deterministic and ensemble consensus supporting multi-\nday rainfall amounts of 1-3+ inches (locally higher). This may \nsupport increasing flooding concerns, including river/stream \nflooding. Furthermore, increasing shear and forcing may also support \na few rounds of strong to severe thunderstorms. There still remains \nquite a bit of uncertainty surrounding finer synoptic details, which \nwill impact the magnitude, location, and timing of heaviest \nrainfall. Stay tuned as we refine forecast details in the coming \ndays.\n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nMostly seasonable to seasonably warm temperatures are forecast \nthrough about Tuesday, switching to seasonably cool to below average \ntemperatures thereafter due to the potential for widespread \nclouds and precipitation.",
